{#-
  ════════ REAL-ESTATE PAIR · BASELINE ASSUMPTIONS, THE SHARED PART ════════

  PR 4f (REAL_ESTATE_PAIR_DESIGN.md §7; rulings M1, P5). Bitcoin vs. Real
  Estate and Bitcoin vs. Rental Property share two assumptions that the site
  keeps for every calculator (shared/modeling-assumptions.js): nominal home
  appreciation and the deflator for the Real view. This file is the one
  source for their words and their preset buttons. Each page lays them out
  in its own idiom (BvRE's fields, BvRP's slider cards) and binds them with
  shared/real-estate-baseline.js (window.RealEstateBaseline), which finds
  them by id: <prefix>ApprPresets, <prefix>HomeAppreciation,
  <prefix>ApprNotice, <prefix>InflPresets, <prefix>Inflation. Preset values
  in the markup are first paint only; the binder rewrites them from
  ModelingAssumptions, so a changed preset is edited in one place (plus the
  prose below, which quotes them: MONTHLY_REFRESH_CHECKLIST §9.3, annual).

  Usage (macros, so each page keeps its own classes):
      {% import 'components/real-estate-baseline.njk' as reb %}
      {{ reb.apprTip('bvre') }}            the help tip, naming the other page
      {{ reb.apprPresets('fwd', 're-preset-btns', 're-preset-btn', 're-preset-val') }}
      {{ reb.inflTip('bvre') }}
      {{ reb.inflPresets('fwd', 're-preset-btns', 're-preset-btn', 're-preset-val') }}
      {{ reb.frameTip('bvre') }}           the Real / Nominal toggle's help tip
  `side` is the page the macro is rendered on: 'bvre' or 'bvrp'.

  The line under the toggle (<prefix>DeflatorLine), which says which frame is
  showing, is written by the binder: its worked example is computed from the
  deflator in force, so this file's static copy states no figure beside it
  (STYLE_GUIDE §10.8).
-#}

{% macro otherPage(side) -%}
{%- if side == 'bvre' -%}Bitcoin vs. Rental Property{%- else -%}Bitcoin vs. Real Estate{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro apprTip(side, extra) -%}
<span class="help-tip{% if extra %} {{ extra }}{% endif %}" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">How fast home prices rise, in <strong>nominal</strong> terms, the way house prices and mortgage rates are quoted. The presets are the recorded US rate to 2025: <strong>Since 2000</strong> 4.68% a year (S&amp;P Cotality Case-Shiller National; the default, and the highest of the three windows), <strong>Since 1990</strong> 4.23%, and <strong>Long run</strong> 3.41% (Shiller&rsquo;s series from 1890, chained to Case-Shiller). A national index: your market can differ. When inflation runs higher, home prices tend to rise in nominal terms while a fixed-rate loan balance doesn&rsquo;t. That is the fixed-rate borrower&rsquo;s inflation benefit. To model a higher-inflation future, raise nominal appreciation. The same setting drives {{ otherPage(side) }}.</span></span>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro inflTip(side, extra) -%}
<span class="help-tip{% if extra %} {{ extra }}{% endif %}" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">The inflation rate the <strong>Real</strong> view uses to turn future dollars into today&rsquo;s. It divides both paths by the same factor, so it never changes which one comes out ahead. <strong>CPI</strong> (3.5%) is about the long-run official rate; <strong>M2 growth</strong> (6.5%), the site&rsquo;s default, is about the 50-year growth of the money supply; <strong>Shadow Stats</strong> (8%) follows John Williams&rsquo;s reconstruction of the pre-1980 CPI method, which is disputed. It is the sitewide setting: changing it here changes it on {{ otherPage(side) }}, The Half-Life and every other calculator that uses it.</span></span>
{%- endmacro %}

{# The Real / Nominal toggle (P5; JM 2026-09-29: say what Real means and how
   it differs from Nominal). No figure: the line under the toggle carries the
   deflator and the worked example. #}
{% macro frameTip(side, extra) -%}
<span class="help-tip{% if extra %} {{ extra }}{% endif %}" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content"><strong>Real</strong> shows each year&rsquo;s dollars in today&rsquo;s purchasing power: divided by the deflator named below, compounded to that year. <strong>Nominal</strong> shows the future dollars themselves, with nothing taken off for inflation. Both paths are divided by the same factor each year, so switching never changes which one is ahead. Money paid or received along the way is nominal in both views, as paid{% if side == 'bvrp' %}, and so are the table&rsquo;s mortgage row and the path card&rsquo;s sale mechanics{% endif %}.</span></span>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro apprPresets(prefix, wrapClass, btnClass, valClass) -%}
<div class="{{ wrapClass }}" id="{{ prefix }}ApprPresets" role="group" aria-label="Home appreciation presets">
    <button type="button" class="{{ btnClass }}" data-preset="long-run" aria-pressed="false">Long run<span class="{{ valClass }}" data-reb-val>3.41%</span></button>
    <button type="button" class="{{ btnClass }}" data-preset="since-1990" aria-pressed="false">Since 1990<span class="{{ valClass }}" data-reb-val>4.23%</span></button>
    <button type="button" class="{{ btnClass }} active" data-preset="since-2000" aria-pressed="true">Since 2000<span class="{{ valClass }}" data-reb-val>4.68%</span></button>
    <button type="button" class="{{ btnClass }}" data-preset="custom" aria-pressed="false">Custom<span class="{{ valClass }}">your own</span></button>
</div>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro inflPresets(prefix, wrapClass, btnClass, valClass) -%}
<div class="{{ wrapClass }}" id="{{ prefix }}InflPresets" role="group" aria-label="Deflator presets">
    <button type="button" class="{{ btnClass }}" data-preset="cpi-official" aria-pressed="false">CPI<span class="{{ valClass }}" data-reb-val>3.5%</span></button>
    <button type="button" class="{{ btnClass }} active" data-preset="m2-growth" aria-pressed="true">M2 growth<span class="{{ valClass }}" data-reb-val>6.5%</span></button>
    <button type="button" class="{{ btnClass }}" data-preset="shadow-stats" aria-pressed="false">Shadow Stats<span class="{{ valClass }}" data-reb-val>8%</span></button>
    <button type="button" class="{{ btnClass }}" data-preset="custom" aria-pressed="false">Custom<span class="{{ valClass }}">your own</span></button>
</div>
{%- endmacro %}

{#- ════════ THE TAX PROFILE (PR 5b; rulings M7, M9, R7) ════════
  Both pages take the same tax inputs, from these macros, so their options
  and words can't drift: the regime, filing status, state and federal
  bracket. Defaults are R7's: United States, married filing jointly, 24%,
  a typical ~5% state. They are page inputs, not sitewide settings (carrying
  them between the pages is PR 8). The state rates here are first paint
  and labels only; the engine's STATE_CAPGAIN holds the numbers. -#}

{% macro regimeOptions(side) -%}
<option value="us" selected>United States</option>
<option value="none">No capital-gains tax</option>
{%- if side == 'bvre' %}
<option value="custom">Custom</option>
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro filingOptions() -%}
<option value="mfj" selected>Married filing jointly</option>
<option value="single">Single</option>
<option value="hoh">Head of household</option>
<option value="mfs">Married filing separately</option>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro stateOptions() -%}
<option value="OTHER" selected>Typical / average (~5%)</option>
<option value="TX">Texas (0%)</option>
<option value="FL">Florida (0%)</option>
<option value="NV">Nevada (0%)</option>
<option value="WA">Washington (0%)</option>
<option value="TN">Tennessee (0%)</option>
<option value="AZ">Arizona (2.5%)</option>
<option value="IN">Indiana (3.0%)</option>
<option value="PA">Pennsylvania (3.07%)</option>
<option value="CO">Colorado (4.4%)</option>
<option value="MI">Michigan (4.25%)</option>
<option value="NC">North Carolina (4.5%)</option>
<option value="IL">Illinois (4.95%)</option>
<option value="MA">Massachusetts (5.0%)</option>
<option value="GA">Georgia (5.39%)</option>
<option value="WI">Wisconsin (7.65%)</option>
<option value="IA">Iowa (8.53%)</option>
<option value="VT">Vermont (8.75%)</option>
<option value="MN">Minnesota (9.85%)</option>
<option value="OR">Oregon (9.9%)</option>
<option value="DC">DC (10.75%)</option>
<option value="NJ">New Jersey (10.75%)</option>
<option value="NY">New York (10.9%)</option>
<option value="HI">Hawaii (11.0%)</option>
<option value="CA">California (13.3%)</option>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro bracketOptions() -%}
<option value="12">12% (lower)</option>
<option value="22">22%</option>
<option value="24" selected>24% (middle)</option>
<option value="32">32%</option>
<option value="35">35%</option>
<option value="37">37% (top)</option>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro regimeTip(side, extra) -%}
<span class="help-tip{% if extra %} {{ extra }}{% endif %}" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content"><strong>United States</strong> applies federal and state tax to the gains on a sale{% if side == 'bvre' %}, with the home-sale exclusion on the house{% else %}, including depreciation recapture on the rental{% endif %}. <strong>No capital-gains tax</strong> leaves every gain untaxed, as in some other countries; income is still taxed{% if side == 'bvrp' %} (rent, lending interest){% endif %}, and property tax is still a cost.</span></span>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro filingTip(extra) -%}
<span class="help-tip{% if extra %} {{ extra }}{% endif %}" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">Sets the home-sale exclusion: up to $500,000 of gain filing jointly, $250,000 otherwise, for a main home owned and lived in for 2 of the last 5 years (IRS Topic 701). The calculator assumes the house is your main home, so it applies from a 2-year horizon.</span></span>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro bracketTip(extra) -%}
<span class="help-tip{% if extra %} {{ extra }}{% endif %}" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">Your ordinary federal bracket. The calculator maps it to a long-term capital-gains rate (0% at 10&ndash;12%, 15% at 22&ndash;32%, 20% at 35&ndash;37%) and applies the 3.8% net investment income tax from the 32% bracket. An approximation: the real rates depend on taxable income and MAGI, and a large gain can push you into a higher band.</span></span>
{%- endmacro %}

{# PR 5c (BvRE): where the bitcoin is held, the mortgage-interest deduction,
   and the Custom regime. Constants here are definitional limits set by
   published acts (STYLE_GUIDE §10.8): IRS News Release on the 2026 limits,
   Publication 936, Rev. Proc. 2025-32. DATA_AUDIT TX-7 to TX-9. #}
{% macro accountTip(extra) -%}
<span class="help-tip{% if extra %} {{ extra }}{% endif %}" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content"><strong>Taxable</strong> (the default): the gain is taxed when the bitcoin is sold. <strong>Tax-advantaged</strong> models a Roth-style account (a Roth IRA holding a bitcoin fund, say): no tax at the sale. Two limits the calculator doesn&rsquo;t apply: contributions are capped (in 2026, $7,500 a year to an IRA, $24,500 to a 401(k)), so the up-front sum usually couldn&rsquo;t go in at once; and a traditional account taxes withdrawals as income instead.</span></span>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro deductionTip(extra) -%}
<span class="help-tip{% if extra %} {{ extra }}{% endif %}" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">Off by default: most households take the standard deduction ($32,200 filing jointly in 2026), and then mortgage interest saves no tax. Turn it on if you itemize. The owner then saves the interest times your federal bracket plus the state rate, on up to $750,000 of loan (IRS Publication 936), and the renter invests that much less each month. It overstates the saving for anyone close to the standard deduction.</span></span>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro customTip(extra) -%}
<span class="help-tip{% if extra %} {{ extra }}{% endif %}" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">Your own rates, for another country or a view of future tax law: a rate on the home&rsquo;s gain after an exemption you set (0 by default), and a rate on bitcoin&rsquo;s gain. Filing status and bracket don&rsquo;t apply, and the mortgage-interest deduction is off.</span></span>
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro notTaxAdvice(cls) -%}
<p class="{{ cls }}">Simplified 2026 federal rules and one rate per state; not tax advice. A CPA can run your actual numbers.</p>
{%- endmacro %}
